---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Reading from '../../layouts/Reading.astro';
import BookSignup from '../../components/BookSignup.astro';
import { book, commitments } from '../../data/book';
import cover from '../../assets/book/cover.png';

/*
  /book/ — public, indexed, and built to do one thing: turn a visit into a
  subscriber. The offer (the opening chapters, free, by email) is above the
  fold beside the cover, and again at the foot of the page for anyone who
  read down first.

  It lives at the root rather than under /new/ because it is being
  promoted now, while the rest of the rebuild is still hidden from search.

  DRAFT COPY. The pitch is assembled from Kevin's own words — the essay
  "Chasing rainbows" (src/content/blog/chasing-rainbows.md) and the about
  page — but the arrangement and the connecting sentences were written for
  this page. Read it before promoting it.
*/

const description = `${book.title} — ${book.subtitle.toLowerCase()}. A book by Kevin Holler. Read ${book.freebie} free.`;
---
<Reading
  title={`${book.title} — Kevin Holler`}
  description={description}
  here="book"
  layout="spread"
  indexed
>
  <div class="spread book-hero">
    <div class="spread-main">
      <p class="book-kicker">A book by Kevin Holler · in progress</p>
      <h1>{book.title}</h1>
      <p class="book-sub">{book.subtitle}</p>

      <p class="lede">
        Building a startup is like chasing a rainbow. It’s a journey full of
        promise and possibility, but the closer you get, the further it seems
        to move.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three years. €50K from friends and family, $250K from Silicon Valley
        investors, and €30K of personal debt to close it down properly. This
        is the story of my first company — what it cost, what it taught me,
        and why I went back and did it again.
      </p>

      <div class="offer" id="chapters">
        <h2>Read {book.freebie} free</h2>
        <p>
          Leave your email and I’ll send them over. You’ll also be the first
          to know when the book is out.
        </p>
        <BookSignup id="top" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="spread-side">
      <figure class="book-cover">
        <Image
          src={cover}
          alt={`Cover of ${book.title}, ${book.subtitle}, by Kevin Holler: the title in heavy black capitals over a row of rainbow-coloured bars that fall away to the right.`}
          widths={[360, 720, 1060]}
          sizes="(max-width: 960px) 70vw, 384px"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="spread">
    <div class="spread-main">
      <h2>What it is</h2>
      <p>
        A little over ten years after graduating in Ireland, I had started
        three companies — two of them failed — worked with five startups at
        varying stages, and somehow ended up writing software, selling it, and
        supporting it, sometimes in the same week.
      </p>
      <p>
        This isn’t a playbook. There are enough of those, and the people who
        write them tend to have won. It’s the other account: what building a
        company costs, what burying one teaches you, and why anyone does it a
        second and a third time.
      </p>

      <h2>Why I’m writing it</h2>
      <p>
        When my first company failed, I wrote a blog post announcing we were
        shutting down. In Ireland at the time, failure was rarely spoken about
        openly. Having already lost everything, I figured I had nothing left
        to lose. I published the post, shut my laptop, and went for a walk.
      </p>
      <p>
        What followed twenty-four hours later shocked me: job offers,
        interview requests, founders emailing to share the same experience.
        The thing I was most afraid to say out loud was the thing other people
        most needed to hear. This book is that post, given the length it
        deserved.
      </p>

      <h2>What it’s held to</h2>
      <dl class="facts">
        {
          commitments.map((c) => (
            <>
              <dt>{c.name}</dt>
              <dd>{c.gloss}</dd>
            </>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      <div class="offer offer-end">
        <h2>Start with {book.freebie}</h2>
        <p>Free, by email. Plus one note when the book launches.</p>
        <BookSignup id="end" />
      </div>

      <p class="stamp book-origin">
        Where it started: the essay <a href="/new/writing/chasing-rainbows/">Chasing rainbows</a>, 2024.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="spread-side"></div>
  </div>
</Reading>
